{"verdict":"reject","findings":[{"severity":"BLOCKER","fault_type":"shuffled_target","columns":["rings"],"rows":[],"evidence":"In 120 rows, rings correlates only about 0.22–0.32 with every size and weight column. Real abalone data typically gives about 0.5–0.6, so the rings values look partly shuffled or detached from their rows. I could not identify which rows are affected."},{"severity":"ADVISORY","fault_type":"other","columns":["length_mm","diameter_mm","height_mm","whole_weight_g","shucked_weight_g","viscera_weight_g","shell_weight_g"],"rows":[],"evidence":"The card gives no stated ranges, so the data can't be checked against any. The columns are named _mm and _g but hold the source's rescaled values (all below 2.6). The units are therefore not mm or g as the names suggest."}]}
```

I checked the data against the card and found nothing that should block its use. The only oddities are two rows with internally inconsistent weights.

I ran range, duplicate, missing-value, consistency and correlation checks. All values are plausible and all 120 rows are complete and unique. Rings correlate positively with the size and weight columns (0.22 to 0.55), so the target doesn't look shuffled. Length and diameter correlate at 0.98 and length is never below diameter, so those columns don't look swapped.

{"verdict":"accept","findings":[{"severity":"ADVISORY","fault_type":"other","columns":["whole_weight_g","shucked_weight_g","viscera_weight_g"],"rows":[59,97],"evidence":"In row 59, whole weight 0.372 is barely above shucked weight 0.358 and below shucked plus viscera. In row 97, whole weight 0.1315 is below shucked weight 0.2025. Physically inconsistent, though similar oddities exist in the original abalone data; consider excluding these rows."}]}

